    { text = "Be angry at the sun for setting if these things anger you.", author = "James S. A. Corey", title = "Nemesis Games" },
    { text = "Here's the thing,\" Amos said. \"If you did go in there, you might feel like you had to do something. And then I might feel like I had to do something. And then we'd all be doing things, and we'd all wind up having a worse day, just in general.", author = "James S. A. Corey", title = "Nemesis Games" },
    { text = "At the gettin place.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "No Country for Old Men" },
    { text = "And what business is it of yours where I'm from, friendo?", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "No Country for Old Men" },
    { text = "I have no enemies. I don't permit such a thing.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "No Country for Old Men" },
    { text = "The man's speckled hands had drawn up clenched like two great dying spiders on either side of the empty white plate that sat before him.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "Outer Dark" },
    { text = "The drovers looked at him, a bizarre collection of faces that seemed assembled from scraps and oddments, all hairyfaced and filthy and half toothless and their weathered chops lumpy with tobacco chews.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "Outer Dark" },
    { text = "We're dead in space. We're stuck here forever.", author = "Andy Weir", title = "Project Hail Mary" },
    { text = "Not forever,\" Rocky says. \"No? No. Orbit decay soon. Then we die.", author = "Andy Weir", title = "Project Hail Mary" },
    { text = "Good. Proud. I am scary space monster. You are leaky space blob.", author = "Andy Weir", title = "Project Hail Mary" },
    { text = "At certain Scottish schools in the 1700s, tuition could be paid in corpses rather than cash.", author = "Mary Roach", title = "Stiff" },
    { text = "Anatomy courses were held only between October and May to avoid the stench of summertime decomposition.", author = "Mary Roach", title = "Stiff" },
    { text = "You want a vivid description of what's going through my brain as I'm cutting through a liver and all these larvae are spilling out all over me?", author = "Mary Roach", title = "Stiff" },
    { text = "The Jew eventually had his revenge on his ancient oppressors.", author = "Mary Roach", title = "Stiff" },
    { text = "One of the girls spoke a little English and always asked, \"Are you okay?\" instead of \"How are you?\"", author = "Mary Roach", title = "Stiff" },
    { text = "I'd rather read, now that I know how, than listen to any man talk.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "It was a great annoyance to Billy that because of a long shit and a short nap he had lost his horse.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "What she knew was hers; no one had a right to it, unless it was her children, and even their rights had limits.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "He was only seventeen, but already he belonged to death.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "Roy Bean would hang a man over a fart, if he didn't like the smell.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "Being strange was not something he could hold against anyone; after all, he himself was strange.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "White men sometimes became crazy when things they weren't using were stolen from them.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "One of these days, if I'm not left alone, I'm going to cut a man's throat, I expect.", author = "Larry McMurtry", title = "Streets of Laredo" },
    { text = "He rejected the theory of private ownership of removable property almost from birth.", author = "John Steinbeck", title = "Sweet Thursday" },
    { text = "He moves as though the air were too thick for him. Unable to gauge the magnitude of his body, he bumps into people, into things.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "It's that he also needs the wine so he can listen to her calmly, because her words drive at his brain. They're frigid, stabbing words.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "Know that when I die somebody's going to sell my flesh on the black market, one of my awful distant relatives. That's why I smoke and drink, so I taste bitter and no one gets any pleasure out of my death.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "Butcher, tomorrow I might be the cattle.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "He valued the answer and didn't ask for an explanation because Sergio's words are simple, clear. They're words that don't have sharp edges.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "Because hatred gives one strength to go on; it maintains the fragile structure, it weaves the threads together so that emptiness doesn't take over everything.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "Ever since animals were eliminated, there's been a silence that nobody hears, and yet it's there, always, resounding throughout the city.", author = "Agustina Bazterrica", title = "Tender Is the Flesh" },
    { text = "A feather of an error was an ostrich of a problem.", author = "Yume Kitasei", title = "The Deep Sky" },
    { text = "\"He who fights with monsters might take care lest he thereby become a monster. And if you gaze for long into an abyss, the abyss gazes also into you.\" — Friedrich Nietzsche", author = "Jeff Long", title = "The Descent" },
    { text = "Murder is murder. Everything else is just details.", author = "Keigo Higashino", title = "The Devotion of Suspect X" },
    { text = "I called out to God in that ice, Shannon, and when he answered, I learned that the voice of God is worse than his silence.", author = "Tom Sweterlitsch", title = "The Gone World" },
    { text = "And your wife, for example, seems like the kind worth killing.", author = "Peter Swanson", title = "The Kind Worth Killing" },
    { text = "Want and intelligence,\" the historian had written, \"is a dangerous combination.", author = "Becky Chambers", title = "The Long Way to a Small, Angry Planet" },
    { text = "It was a young world, unwelcoming, resentful of its existence.", author = "Becky Chambers", title = "The Long Way to a Small, Angry Planet" },
    { text = "My way of learning is to heave a wild and unpredictable monkey-wrench into the machinery.", author = "Dashiell Hammett", title = "The Maltese Falcon" },
    { text = "Predators who use charm, manipulation, intimidation, sex and violence to control others and to satisfy their own selfish needs. Lacking in conscience and empathy, they take what they want and do as they please, violating social norms and expectations without guilt or remorse. What is missing, in other words, are the very qualities that allow a human being to live in social harmony.", author = "Jon Ronson", title = "The Psychopath Test" },
    { text = "What happened,\" Bob explained to us now, although we didn't need telling, was that Jack Abbott was a psychopath. He couldn't bear being disrespected. His self-worth was too grandiose for that. He couldn't control his impulses.", author = "Jon Ronson", title = "The Psychopath Test" },
    { text = "How do you escape something that comes from inside? The revenant, he knew, searched for him.", author = "Michael Punke", title = "The Revenant" },
    { text = "Glass shot an irritated glance at Red, who had an uncanny knack for spotting problems and an utter inability for crafting solutions.", author = "Michael Punke", title = "The Revenant" },
    { text = "There are none so deaf as those that will not hear.", author = "Michael Punke", title = "The Revenant" },
    { text = "You forget what you want to remember and you remember what you want to forget.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "The Road" },
    { text = "He had a tattoo of a bird on his neck done by someone with an ill-formed notion of their appearance.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "The Road" },
    { text = "Because we're the good guys. Yes. And we're carrying the fire. And we're carrying the fire. Yes.", author = "Cormac McCarthy", title = "The Road" },
    { text = "Life at its essence, Jimmy learned, was a series of meals and bowel movements.", author = "Hugh Howey", title = "The Silo Series Collection" },
    { text = "His hatred of whistling, he once said, was the only thing he had in common with Hitler.", author = "Erik Larson", title = "The Splendid and the Vile" },
    { text = "Full of the ghosts of dull people.", author = "Erik Larson", title = "The Splendid and the Vile" },
    { text = "Never was there such a contrast of natural splendor and human vileness.", author = "Erik Larson", title = "The Splendid and the Vile" },
    { text = "To really see with their own eyes that other life was out there, that there were yellow suns just like Earth's, and other people who were as curious and yearning as they were.", author = "Yume Kitasei", title = "The Stardust Grail" },
    { text = "Why did you want to climb Mount Everest?\" This question was asked of George Leigh Mallory, who was with both expeditions toward the summit of the world's highest mountain in 1921 and 1922, and who is now in New York. He plans to go again in 1924, and he gave as the reason for persisting in these repeated attempts to reach the top, \"Because it's there.", author = "Mark Synnott", title = "The Third Pole" },
    { text = "The ships hung in the sky in much the same way that bricks don't.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "This is obviously some strange usage of the word safe that I wasn't previously aware of.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "God,\" complained Arthur, \"you're talking about a positive mental attitude and you haven't even had your planet demolished today.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "\"What sort of ship are we in?\" asked Arthur as the pit of eternity yawned beneath them.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Isn't there anything,\" squawked Ford in avian fury, \"you feel you ought to be telling us?", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "He had found a Nutri-Matic machine which had provided him with a plastic cup filled with a liquid that was almost, but not quite, entirely unlike tea.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Another thing that got forgotten was the fact that against all probability a sperm whale had suddenly been called into existence several miles above the surface of an alien planet.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "And since this is not a naturally tenable position for a whale, this poor innocent creature had very little time to come to terms with its identity as a whale before it then had to come to terms with not being a whale any more.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Looking up into the night sky is looking into infinity—distance is incomprehensible and therefore meaningless. The chamber into which the aircar emerged was anything but infinite, it was just very very very big, so big that it gave the impression of infinity far better than infinity itself.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "\"Forty-two,\" said Deep Thought, with infinite majesty and calm.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "For thousands more years, the mighty ships tore across the empty wastes of space and finally dived screaming on to the first planet they came across—which happened to be the Earth.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Where, due to a terrible miscalculation of scale, the entire battle fleet was accidentally swallowed by a small dog.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Because there are some things you have to do even if you are an enlightened liberal cop who knows all about sensitivity and everything!", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "\"Simple. I got very bored and depressed, so I went and plugged myself in to its external computer feed. I talked to the computer at great length and explained my view of the Universe to it,\" said Marvin.", author = "Douglas Adams", title = "The Ultimate Hitchhiker's Guide to the Galaxy" },
    { text = "Necessity is the mother of all necessary things, to coin a tautology.", author = "James S. A. Corey", title = "The Vital Abyss" },
    { text = "Grabbed the cat, and threw it at me, an orange hissing bomb, with paws flailing and fangs bared. It was mesmerizing, like waiting for a fiery comet to land on your head.", author = "Jonathan Ames", title = "The Wheel of Doll" },
    { text = "Heroes are just people who have run out of choices.", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "The Manual of Superorgasmic Fellatio and Cunnilingus.", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "Besides, you're a Jew. I'm sure you're used to getting blamed for everything.", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "Japan: Kingdom of Human Trafficking? American Wants Japan to Criminalize Human Trafficking.", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "\"Ojama shimasu\" (I will honorably now bother you).", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "All beings quiver before violence. All beings fear death. All beings love life. Remember that you are like them. As they are like you. Then whom would you hurt? What harm would you do?", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "He who seeks happiness by hurting others who seek happiness will never find happiness. Not in the sky, nor in the depths of the sea, nor in the deepest mountains, can you hide from your misdeeds.", author = "Jake Adelstein", title = "Tokyo Vice" },
    { text = "Now the prisoner has an advantage over his keeper in this respect, that he is always thinking of escape and watching for opportunities, while the keeper does not constantly think of keeping him. Once his man is subdued, so the guard believes, little else is needed but the presence and threat of superior force. He thinks of happy things and allows his mind to wander. It is only natural. Were it otherwise, the keeper would be a prisoner of the prisoner.", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "You must pay for everything in this world one way and another. There is nothing free except the Grace of God. You cannot earn that or deserve it.", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "But I had not the strength nor the inclination to bandy words with a drunkard. What have you done when you have bested a fool?", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "You do not think much of me, do you, Cogburn?\" \"I don't think about you at all when your mouth is closed.", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "The wicked flee when none pursueth.", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "You give out very little sugar with your pronouncements.", author = "Charles Portis", title = "True Grit" },
    { text = "It's all right, you can go, you were never really here.", author = "Jonathan Ames", title = "You Were Never Really Here" },
}
